# frozen_string_literal: true

# Generated by shipshape. This file is yours now.
#
# **The only place in the application that names a job.** `perform_later` appears here and in
# the writing doors' `call_later`, both under `app/shipshape/`, which every cop excludes — so
# application code never names a job class and the rule "nothing may call an entry point"
# stays absolute. The capability arrives underneath the call graph rather than through a hole
# cut in it.
#
# One command, one transaction, one job. A retry therefore re-runs exactly one transaction,
# which is the grain at which `a-command-runs-twice` makes deferral safe. There is no job for
# a workflow: it spans several transactions, and one job holding three of them would retry
# steps that already committed.
class OperationJob < ActiveJob::Base
  # Used when an operation declares no `RETRIES` of its own.
  DEFAULT_ATTEMPTS = 5

  # **A raise is the retry signal. A `failure` is not** — it is expected and terminal, the
  # door has already recorded it in the audit log, and retrying it would repeat a decision
  # that will not change.
  #
  # **The retry is written out rather than declared with `retry_on`**, and that is what makes
  # the limit per-operation. `retry_on` captures its `attempts:` when the class is defined, so
  # one job class would mean one policy for every command; `retry_job` is the same mechanism
  # with the number read at the moment it is needed. `executions` is what ActiveJob has
  # already counted, so nothing here keeps its own tally.
  def perform(operation:, arguments: {}, actor_class: nil, actor_id: nil)
    Object.const_get(operation).call(**actor(actor_class, actor_id), **keywords(arguments))
  rescue StandardError => error
    raise error if executions >= attempts_for(operation)

    retry_job(wait: backoff)
  end

  # **How a deferred run finds the actor again.** Only the application knows what an actor is,
  # so this is the one thing it has to say. The default is what a Rails application means by
  # it; replace the lambda if yours differs.
  #
  #   OperationJob.actor_finder = ->(class_name, id) { Person.active.find(id) }
  def self.actor_finder
    @actor_finder ||= ->(class_name, id) { Object.const_get(class_name).find(id) }
  end

  class << self
    attr_writer :actor_finder
  end

  private

  # `RETRIES = 2` on the operation, read at the moment the decision is made rather than when
  # this class was defined. `false` so an operation does not inherit a limit somebody set for
  # a different one.
  def attempts_for(operation)
    klass = Object.const_get(operation)

    klass.const_defined?(:RETRIES, false) ? klass.const_get(:RETRIES, false) : DEFAULT_ATTEMPTS
  end

  def backoff
    (executions**2) + 1
  end

  def actor(class_name, id)
    return {} if class_name.nil? || id.nil?

    { actor: self.class.actor_finder.call(class_name, id) }
  end

  # ActiveJob hands back string keys, and any `Shape` among the arguments arrives packed. The
  # doors take named keywords and assert them, so both have to be undone before the call —
  # `ShapePacking.unpack` does both, because symbolising is what it does to every Hash it is
  # not told is a shape.
  def keywords(arguments)
    ShapePacking.unpack(arguments)
  end
end
